WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.680
Good morning John, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:06.370
I am right now on the set of Wil Wheaton's
TableTop RPG show.

00:00:06.370 --> 00:00:12.000
I have been having an amazing time here going
on adventures in a custom designed campaign

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:16.230
that Wil made for me, Yuri Lowenthal, Laura
Bailey, and Alison Haislip.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:19.860
John, I've been discovering the weirdnesses
of Snapchat and I decided to do a question

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.390
video today, and the questions are all gonna
come from Snapchat.

00:00:23.390 --> 00:00:24.810
What did you play with Wil?

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:29.570
We've been playing a brand new RPG that did
not exist before but it's called Titansgrave:

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:30.570
The Ashes of Valkana.

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:33.410
It's sort of sci-fi mixed with fantasy world.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:36.720
If you could remove an element from the periodic
table, which one would you choose?

00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:41.860
I assume we're talking about removing an element
of the periodic table from the world because

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:44.670
you can't just remove an element from the
periodic table, that doesn't do anything;

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:46.600
it's just less knowledge that we have then.

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:48.899
I think I'd probably pick one of the more
dangerous ones.

00:00:48.899 --> 00:00:53.489
Like if we had less plutonium in the world,
I don't know that that would be a bad thing.

00:00:53.489 --> 00:00:55.030
What is your songwriting process?

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:59.380
Usually I start with like one good line that
I really love that has really great rhythm

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.460
and that I can kind of start a melody around.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:07.549
If I love that line enough, then it gives
me the desire to work hard to create a song

00:01:07.549 --> 00:01:13.939
around it, so it's more how do I inspire myself
to want to write a song than "how do I actually

00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:14.939
write the song?"

00:01:14.939 --> 00:01:17.599
'Cause if I want it bad enough I feel like
it's just gonna happen.

00:01:17.599 --> 00:01:19.450
How are you liking Snapchat so far?

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:20.450
That's a lovely elephant!

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:21.450
Snapchat is weird.

00:01:21.450 --> 00:01:23.299
I don't think that I use it the way that most
people use it.

00:01:23.299 --> 00:01:27.399
Because, I, you know, I already have an audience,
and so I'm kind of just trying to make people

00:01:27.399 --> 00:01:31.549
laugh and have a good time, and it's a really
great format for that because I don't have

00:01:31.549 --> 00:01:35.799
to worry about the things being, like, part
of my permanent record.

00:01:35.799 --> 00:01:40.079
I feel more free to just mess around and be
dumb, which is important to me.

00:01:40.079 --> 00:01:41.079
Favorite national park?

00:01:41.079 --> 00:01:42.219
I'm gonna go with Yellow - ooh!

00:01:42.219 --> 00:01:44.189
I kicked a die box!

00:01:44.189 --> 00:01:45.189
Sorry dice!

00:01:45.189 --> 00:01:46.770
- I'm gonna go with Yellowstone National Park
'cause it is weird!

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:50.929
It is an amazing magical place; it's got a
lot of geological weirdness and biological

00:01:50.929 --> 00:01:51.929
weirdness.

00:01:51.929 --> 00:01:53.340
How cute is my banana monkey?

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.909
Your banana monkey is cute; I am worried about
where its legs are.

00:01:56.909 --> 00:01:59.659
Who do you normally play as on Mario Kart?

00:01:59.659 --> 00:02:02.819
I'm a Donkey Kong man, uh, Mario Kart and
Smash Brothers.

00:02:02.819 --> 00:02:04.479
I just need the extra beef.

00:02:04.479 --> 00:02:06.049
I'm not a finesse player.

00:02:06.049 --> 00:02:07.859
What's your favorite kitchen utensil?

00:02:07.859 --> 00:02:10.560
That is a weird question!

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:12.019
I - maybe a colander?

00:02:12.019 --> 00:02:13.980
Why do dogs get eye boogers too?

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:16.380
Dogs get eye boogers for the same reason we
do.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:19.690
There's stuff that comes out of your eye -- it's
usually, it's dissolved in your tears.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:22.909
It's called rheum, and it contains all kinds
of stuff you probably don't want to know about

00:02:22.909 --> 00:02:27.409
like blood cells and epithelial cells, um,
and also mucus.

00:02:27.409 --> 00:02:31.420
When the water leaves mucus, the stuff it
leaves behind is the mucan compounds, which

00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:34.200
are proteins, which are sort of gummy and
gross.

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:37.950
And dogs have just as much of that stuff as
we do; I think most mammals or all mammals,

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:40.900
ah, even get that goin' on.

00:02:40.900 --> 00:02:43.300
Best Buy employees love SciShow apparently.

00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:45.849
That's not a question, but thank you for letting
me know!

00:02:45.849 --> 00:02:46.849
That's cool.

00:02:46.849 --> 00:02:47.849
LASAGNA CUPCAKES!

00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:49.379
Yeah, also not a question!

00:02:49.379 --> 00:02:53.110
If there are two dogs and one cat, how many
fish are there?

00:02:53.110 --> 00:02:56.080
As far as I can tell, the number of dogs and
cats has no bearing on the number of fish.

00:02:56.080 --> 00:03:02.870
Why haven't you done a dance vid in a while?

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:07.780
[music]

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:15.180
John, I'm really excited about this show;
it's gonna be coming out in June so it's gonna

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.540
be a while before everybody gets to see it.

00:03:16.540 --> 00:03:21.599
But we have been having an amazing time with
these little dice, and I'm excited to share

00:03:21.599 --> 00:03:22.750
it with all of you!

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:24.409
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

